items/lem-rat-cut-embeds.md-86-
items/lem-rat-cut-embeds.md-87-2.2 Nonnegative multiplicative identity: for $p, q > 0$ the two inclusions give $(pq)^{*} = p^{*} \cdot q^{*}$, while if $p = 0$ or $q = 0$ then $pq = 0$ and the sign rule $0^{*} \cdot B = 0^{*}$ gives $(pq)^{*} = 0^{*} = p^{*} \cdot q^{*}$; hence $(pq)^{*} = p^{*} \cdot q^{*}$ for all $p, q \ge 0$. [step 1.6, step 1.7, L5]
items/lem-rat-cut-embeds.md-88-
items/lem-rat-cut-embeds.md:89:2.3 Injectivity: if $p^{*} = q^{*}$ then neither $p^{*} \subsetneq q^{*}$ nor $q^{*} \subsetneq p^{*}$, so by reflection $\neg(p < q)$ and $\neg(q < p)$; trichotomy forces $p = q$. [step 1.2, L3]
items/lem-rat-cut-embeds.md-90-
items/lem-rat-cut-embeds.md:91:2.4 Combining preservation and reflection, $p < q \iff p^{*} \subsetneq q^{*}$, that is $p < q \iff p^{*} < q^{*}$: the embedding preserves and reflects order. [step 1.1, step 1.2, L2]
items/lem-rat-cut-embeds.md-92-
items/lem-rat-cut-embeds.md-93-2.5 $A < y^{*}$: for $a \in A$, separation gives $a < x$ (as $x \notin A$) and $x < y$, so $a < y$ and $a \in y^{*}$, whence $A \subseteq y^{*}$; and $x \in y^{*}$ (since $x < y$) while $x \notin A$, so the inclusion is proper, $A \subsetneq y^{*}$. [step 1.8, L1, L2, L3]
