items/thm-linearity-of-the-integral.md-68-
items/thm-linearity-of-the-integral.md-69-[L6] A supremum is the least upper bound and an infimum the greatest lower bound; both exist for a nonempty bounded set and are unique ([[def-complete-ordered-field]], [[def-infimum]], [[lem-sup-unique]]).
items/thm-linearity-of-the-integral.md-70-
items/thm-linearity-of-the-integral.md:71:[L7] Reflection: writing $-S = \{-s : s \in S\}$, a real $u$ is an upper bound of $S$ exactly when $-u$ is a lower bound of $-S$, and conversely; hence $\sup(-S) = -\inf S$ and $\inf(-S) = -\sup S$ for nonempty bounded $S$, by [L6] ([[lem-reflection]]).
items/thm-linearity-of-the-integral.md-72-
items/thm-linearity-of-the-integral.md-73-[L8] The constant function $0$ is integrable with $\int_a^b 0 = 0$ ([[lem-integral-elementary-bounds]]).
items/thm-linearity-of-the-integral.md-74-
